<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Takeda Shingen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1521">1521</a>–<a href="/years/1573">1573</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Takeda Shingen was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> who ruled Kai province and expanded into Shinano and Suruga. He fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a> in the battles of Kawanakajima and clashed with the Imagawa and Hōjō. In 1573 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> at Mikatagahara but died soon after, and his heir was crushed at Nagashino in 1575.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen.jpg" alt="Takeda Shingen" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Takeda_Harunobu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the plain of Mikatagahara north of Hamamatsu, <b>Takeda Shingen</b>'s army encircled the Tokugawa-Oda force and almost totally annihilated it; many of Ieyasu's retainers were killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-25">25 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Ujizane lost Sunpu to <b>Takeda Shingen</b> in 1568 and surrendered Kakegawa castle to Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1569, ending the domain.</blockquote><a href="/state/imagawa-clan-domain">Imagawa clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Hojo Ujiyasu won a celebrated victory at Kawagoe in 1546 and expanded across the Kanto, resisting attacks by Uesugi Kenshin and <b>Takeda Shingen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-hojo-clan-domain">Later Hojo clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1541 his son <b>Harunobu</b>, later known as Shingen, exiled him and took over.</blockquote><a href="/state/takeda-clan-domain">Takeda clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>He survived defeat by <b>Takeda Shingen</b> at Mikatagahara in 1573 and gained Suruga, Kai and part of Shinano after 1582.</blockquote><a href="/state/tokugawa-clan-domain">Tokugawa clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Kenshin campaigned almost every year, in the Kanto against the Later Hojo and in Shinano against <b>Takeda Shingen</b>, with five battles at Kawanakajima, and later in Etchu and Noto.</blockquote><a href="/state/uesugi-clan-domain">Uesugi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yogaisan Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Ina district. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.7,138.6&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Yogaisan Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8050 874 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Takeda Shingen"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8500 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8500 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.7,138.6&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
